---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import CaseStudyNav from '../../components/CaseStudyNav.astro';
import discoveryDelivery from '../../assets/images/product-management-discovery-delivery.png';
import miroIdeationCritique from '../../assets/images/miro-ideation-critique-example.png';
import waysOfWorking from '../../assets/images/way-of-working-2.png';
import miroIdeation from '../../assets/images/miro-ideation-example.png';
import flowDiagram from '../../assets/images/flow-diagram-2.png';
import figmaFlows from '../../assets/images/figma-myaccount-flows.png';
import travelWireframes from '../../assets/images/travel-sd-wireframes.png';
import raaWires from '../../assets/images/raa-wires.png';
import usertesting from '../../assets/images/usertesting-2.png';
import figmaRenewals from '../../assets/images/figma-renewals-example.png';
import myAccount from '../../assets/images/my-account.png';
import uxBenchmarking from '../../assets/images/ux-benchmarking.png';
import journeyMap1 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-1.png';
import orchestration from '../../assets/images/claude-orchestration-layer-diagram.png';
---
<Layout title="UX & Product Process | Andrew Smith" description="How experience design works within the product delivery cycle — from early roadmap conversations through discovery, design, delivery, and measurement.">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>UX & Product Process</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">How experience design works within the product delivery cycle.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">The experience design team works as part of the product trio — alongside product management and engineering — from the earliest conversations about what is worth building through to measuring whether it worked. This is what that contribution looks like at each stage, and how I led the team through it as manager while contributing directly as a practitioner.</p>
        <p class="hero-subtext">Covering discovery, design, delivery, and measurement across multiple product squads simultaneously.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={discoveryDelivery} alt="Dual-track agile diagram showing discovery track focusing on value, usability, feasibility, and viability alongside delivery track focusing on scalability, reliability, performance, and maintainability" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
        <p class="diagram-caption">Discovery and delivery as parallel tracks — the model that shapes how experience design and product work together.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="process-overview">
        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">01</span><span class="step-label">Roadmap & Vision</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">02</span><span class="step-label">Analytics & Insights</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">03</span><span class="step-label">Discovery</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">04</span><span class="step-label">Workshops</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">05</span><span class="step-label">Flow & IA</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">06</span><span class="step-label">Design & Test</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">07</span><span class="step-label">Delivery</span></div>
          <div class="step-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="step"><span class="step-num">08</span><span class="step-label">Measure</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">Operating Model</span>
          <h2>Aligning the team's process to the product delivery cycle.</h2>
          <p>Before any piece of work begins, the team has a shared framework for how it moves through discovery and delivery. Three pathways: dual-track discovery for problems that need defining before any solution is considered, solution discovery for problems already understood, and existing components for work the design system already solves.</p>
          <p>This structure means design is always working ahead of delivery, not catching up to it. Product managers and engineering leads know what the team is working on and when it will feed into the squad. The stages below describe what happens at each point in that process.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={waysOfWorking} alt="Ways of working framework with pathway decision panel open showing dual-track discovery, solution discovery, and existing components pathways" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">01</span>
          <h2>Roadmap & Vision Alignment</h2>
          <p>Before any piece of design work begins, the team attends roadmap and vision discussions with product management and senior leadership. The goal is to understand what the business is trying to achieve, what product is prioritising and why, and where the experience design team can contribute ahead of squads picking up work.</p>
          <p>As manager, this is where I represented the team at the senior management level — working with the senior manager of product, service design, and engineering to align the team's discovery and delivery capacity with upcoming roadmap priorities across multiple product streams.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={miroIdeationCritique} alt="FigJam discovery session showing problem framing, competitor analysis, and design options" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">02</span>
          <h2>Analytics & Insights Review</h2>
          <p>The team reviews product analytics alongside the insights function and product managers before scoping any discovery work. Drop-off rates, conversion data, feature adoption, NPS and CSAT trends — understanding what the data is showing informs where discovery investment will have the most impact.</p>
          <p>This is not a UX activity done in isolation. It is a shared review with product and insights to agree on what the data is telling us and where the gaps in understanding are. The <a href="/portfolio/work/journey-mapping">journey map</a> captures this alongside qualitative research, connecting quantitative evidence to specific stages of the experience.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing live metrics, analytics, and qualitative insights connected to each stage of the experience" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">03</span>
          <h2>Discovery</h2>
          <p>Discovery is where the team moves from understanding what the data shows to understanding why. User research, usability testing, competitor analysis, and customer feedback from the contact centre are synthesised to build a clear picture of the problem before any solution is considered.</p>
          <p>Discovery runs continuously alongside delivery — it is not a phase that ends. Findings feed back into the journey map and into planning conversations with product about what to prioritise next. AI tools assist with synthesis, surfacing patterns across sessions faster and identifying where knowledge gaps exist before they become expensive problems in delivery.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={miroIdeation} alt="Miro board showing current state analysis, annotated observations, and design options" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">04</span>
          <h2>Workshops & Design Sprints</h2>
          <p>The team facilitates workshops and design sprints with product managers, engineers, service designers, and business stakeholders to align on problems, generate options, and make decisions about direction. Creating the workshop materials, running the sessions, and synthesising the outputs is part of what the experience design team brings to the product process.</p>
          <p>As manager, I planned and led these sessions at the program level — working across squads to run discovery workshops that fed into roadmap planning rather than individual feature delivery. The goal is always the same: get the right people in the room with the right information, and leave with a shared decision rather than a list of options to revisit later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={miroIdeation} alt="FigJam discovery and ideation session with stakeholders" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">05</span>
          <h2>Flow & Information Architecture</h2>
          <p>Every path through the experience mapped before any screen is designed. Decision points, edge cases, and alternate flows documented alongside product managers and engineers so that requirements and design are aligned before development scope is committed.</p>
          <p>A gap found in a flow diagram costs an afternoon to fix. The same gap found in a coded feature costs a sprint. Getting this right early is one of the clearest ways the design team adds value to the product delivery cycle.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={figmaFlows} alt="Figma high-level user flow and information architecture" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">06</span>
          <h2>Design & Testing</h2>
          <p>Wireframes before high fidelity. Usability testing before build. The <a href="/portfolio/work/design-systems">design system</a> handles the repeatable patterns so testing time focuses on the genuinely new interactions — the decisions that have not been made before and carry the most risk if they are wrong.</p>
          <p>Finished designs include accessibility guidance, content notes, and edge case handling alongside the visual design. Engineers have what they need to build without interpretation. AI assists with spec generation and component documentation, reducing the manual overhead that would otherwise compete with design and testing time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={travelWireframes} alt="Travel booking wireframes showing search, detail, payment, and confirmation screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
          <Image class="process-img" src={usertesting} alt="UserTesting session showing participant screen with tagged transcript" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
          <Image class="process-img" src={myAccount} alt="Shipped product screens showing authenticated member portal" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">07</span>
          <h2>Delivery & Agile Ceremonies</h2>
          <p>The team works within Agile SCRUM delivery — sprint planning, backlog refinement, sprint reviews, and retrospectives. The designer's role in these ceremonies is to keep the squad aligned on what is being built, flag when implementation is drifting from the design intent, and contribute to scope decisions alongside the product manager and engineering lead.</p>
          <p>As manager, I was responsible for the team's delivery cadence across multiple squads simultaneously — tracking progress, resolving blockers, and ensuring the ways of working between design and engineering were producing the right outcomes rather than just keeping the process moving.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={figmaRenewals} alt="Figma canvas showing design screens across desktop and mobile" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">08</span>
          <h2>Measurement</h2>
          <p>Shipping is where the hypothesis gets tested at scale. <a href="/portfolio/work/research-benchmarking">Benchmarking</a> runs at regular intervals after delivery — tracking effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability so the data tells a story about direction over time. Results feed back into the journey map and into the next planning cycle with product.</p>
          <p>The loop closes here. What the measurement reveals becomes the input to the next round of analytics review and discovery planning. This is what makes design a continuous contribution to the product rather than a one-time delivery.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={uxBenchmarking} alt="Usability benchmarking dashboard showing effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability metrics" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="process-section">
        <div class="process-label">
          <span class="step-tag">AI</span>
          <h2>AI in the Process</h2>
          <p>AI tools are now part of how the team works at multiple stages — from research synthesis and journey map generation in discovery, to spec documentation and accessibility checking in delivery. The goal is reducing the overhead that competes with the work that requires judgment, not replacing the judgment itself.</p>
          <p>The orchestration layer built for the team connected the design system, journey maps, research repository, and live product data into a single queryable source — so knowledge was accessible across a distributed team without manual search across four tools. See the <a href="/portfolio/work/ai-tooling">AI Tooling</a> page for the full picture.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-images">
          <Image class="process-img" src={orchestration} alt="Experience Design operating model showing AI orchestration layer connecting all tools and knowledge sources" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:860px) 100vw, 60vw"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <CaseStudyNav />
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
.lead-image-section{padding:2rem 0 3rem}
.lead-image{width:100%;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:1.5rem;box-shadow:0 16px 40px rgba(23,23,23,.08)}
.diagram-caption{font-size:.85rem;color:var(--muted-foreground);font-style:italic;margin:.75rem 0 0;text-align:center}
.case-page{position:relative;width:min(calc(100% - 3rem),78rem);margin:0 auto;padding:1.25rem 0 5rem}
.eyebrow{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.82rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 1.25rem;display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center}
.breadcrumb-link{font-weight:600}.breadcrumb-separator{color:var(--muted-foreground)}
h1,h2{margin:0;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.05em;color:var(--foreground)}
.display-title{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.5rem);line-height:1.06;margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:28ch}
.hero-text{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .5rem;max-width:42rem}
.hero-subtext{font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--muted-foreground);font-style:italic;margin:.25rem 0 0;max-width:42rem}
.case-hero{padding:5rem 0 2rem}
.process-overview{padding:2rem 0 3rem;border-top:1px solid var(--border);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.process-steps{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:.5rem}
.step{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:.25rem;padding:.75rem 1rem;border-radius:.5rem;background:var(--muted);min-width:5rem;text-align:center}
.step-num{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.7rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted-foreground)}
.step-label{font-size:.85rem;font-weight:700}
.step-arrow{color:var(--muted-foreground);font-size:1.2rem}
.process-section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:22rem minmax(0,1fr);gap:3rem;padding:3rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.process-label h2{font-size:1.3rem;margin:.5rem 0;letter-spacing:-.02em}
.process-label p{font-size:.96rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:.75rem 0 0}
.process-label a{color:var(--foreground);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px;text-decoration-color:var(--border)}
.process-label a:hover{text-decoration-color:var(--foreground)}
.step-tag{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.72rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground)}
.process-images{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.25rem}
.process-img{width:100%;border-radius:1rem;border:1px solid var(--border);box-shadow:0 8px 20px rgba(23,23,23,.07)}
@media(max-width:860px){.meta-strip{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1rem}.process-section{grid-template-columns:1fr}.process-steps{gap:.35rem}.step{min-width:4rem;padding:.5rem .75rem}}
</style>
